{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I’m Dr. Lee. I’ll be your doctor during this stay. You came in today because of shortness of breath. Can you tell me more about what’s been going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, thank you. I’ve been feeling worse over the last few days. I can’t walk more than 20 feet without getting winded. Before, I could do about 150 feet."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. And any trouble breathing when lying down?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I’ve had to sleep propped up. It’s been getting worse for a few nights now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. I also see your scooter broke, and you haven’t been able to get to your clinic or take your medications for a few weeks. Is that right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, exactly. I’ve been stuck in bed. I only started taking my meds again a few days ago."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That makes sense. We think you’re retaining fluid, which is making it harder to breathe. Your weight is up, and we heard crackles in your lungs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, okay. So it’s not my lungs or heart failing again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not a heart attack—your troponin was normal. But your heart failure is flaring up because of the fluid. We’ll give you IV diuretics to help you lose the extra fluid."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that sounds good. I’ve been feeling swollen in my thighs too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, that’s likely fluid buildup. We’ll monitor your weight and urine output closely. We also did a chest X-ray, but it was hard to see clearly because of the technique, but no signs of pneumonia or severe fluid overload."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. What about my INR? I stopped warfarin when I stopped my meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good question. Your INR was 1.0 when you came in—too low. You’re at risk for clots. We’ve started a heparin drip to bridge you back to warfarin."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, I didn’t realize it was that low. Is that dangerous?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2148-11-14 17:29:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It increases your risk for another clot, yes. But we’re on it. We’ll keep the heparin going until your INR is therapeutic again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2148-11-15 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs from this morning show your INR is starting to rise, and your kidney function is stable. You’ve had good urine output overnight—about 3 liters."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2148-11-15 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a lot! I do feel a bit less bloated."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2148-11-15 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s great. We’re going to keep the IV lasix twice daily for now. Also, your cortisol level was very low this morning—4 mcg/dL. That’s likely from long-term steroid use suppressing your adrenal glands."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2148-11-15 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that why I’ve been so weak and tired?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2148-11-15 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Partly. The steroids are still important for your polymyositis, so we’re continuing them. But we’ll be careful not to stop them suddenly. Physical therapy will see you today to assess your strength."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2148-11-15 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ve been feeling really weak. I haven’t moved much in weeks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2148-11-16 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. You’re down about 5 kilograms since admission—great progress. Your breathing is better, and lungs are clearing."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2148-11-16 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I do feel better. I even sat in the chair for 20 minutes yesterday with PT."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2148-11-16 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s excellent. PT thinks you’d benefit from inpatient rehab to rebuild strength before going home. You’re still quite deconditioned."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2148-11-16 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Rehab? I was hoping to go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2148-11-16 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I understand. But given how long you’ve been bedbound and your baseline weakness from polymyositis, rehab will help you regain independence safely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2148-11-16 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, if it’ll help me get stronger, I’ll do it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2148-11-17 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your INR is now 2.1—perfect. We’ve stopped the heparin and will keep you on warfarin 6 mg daily, same as before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2148-11-17 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great! Do I need to get it checked after I leave?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2148-11-17 08:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, daily at first, until it’s stable. Your care team at rehab will help with that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2148-11-17 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, good to know."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2148-11-18 09:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning to switch you from IV lasix to oral torsemide tomorrow—100 mg daily. That’s half your previous dose, but you’ve lost a lot of fluid."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2148-11-18 09:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I was on 200 mg before. Is 100 enough?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2148-11-18 09:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "For now, yes. We’ll monitor your weight and symptoms. If you start retaining fluid again, we can adjust it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2148-11-18 09:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, makes sense."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2148-11-18 09:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Also, your blood sugar has been running high—up to 189. We held metformin in the hospital, but we’ll restart it at discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2148-11-18 09:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been on insulin shots while here, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2148-11-18 09:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, sliding scale insulin. But since your sugars are manageable, we’ll go back to your oral meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2148-11-19 10:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Final day on the unit. Your weight is 211.8 kg—down from 223.2 kg. Lungs are clear, and you’re breathing well on room air."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2148-11-19 10:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s a big drop! I feel so much better."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2148-11-19 10:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ve done great. We’re discharging you tomorrow to a rehab facility. They’ll help you regain strength and make sure your meds are managed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2148-11-19 10:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay. What should I watch for when I’m at rehab?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2148-11-19 10:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Keep an eye on your weight. If you gain more than 3 pounds in a day or two, let someone know. Also, watch for shortness of breath or swelling."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2148-11-19 10:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my INR checks?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2148-11-19 10:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "They’ll check it daily at first. Same with your electrolytes, since diuretics can affect potassium."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2148-11-19 10:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m on potassium pills too, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2148-11-19 10:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, Klor-Con daily. And all your other meds—steroids, mycophenolate, omeprazole, citalopram—are continuing as before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2148-11-19 10:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good. I won’t stop them again, I promise."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2148-11-19 10:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I’m glad to hear that. Staying on your meds is key to avoiding another hospitalization."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2148-11-20 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Time to go. You’re doing well—clear lungs, stable vitals, and much improved strength. Rehab will help you get back on your feet."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2148-11-20 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Dr. Lee. I feel so much better than when I came in."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2148-11-20 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Remember: take your meds, weigh yourself daily, and call if you gain weight or feel short of breath."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2148-11-20 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. And I’ll get my scooter fixed too!"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2148-11-20 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s a great plan. Take care, and good luck at rehab."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2148-11-14 17:08:00",
    "admission_end": "2148-11-20 15:33:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with a history of polymyositis, diastolic heart failure, morbid obesity, and anticoagulation for prior clots was admitted for worsening shortness of breath and fluid overload after being unable to access her medications due to a broken scooter. She had stopped her medications, including warfarin and diuretics, for several weeks. She was treated with IV diuresis, which improved her respiratory status and reduced her weight from 223.2 kg to 211.8 kg. Her INR was initially 1.0, so she was bridged with heparin and restarted on warfarin, reaching a therapeutic INR of 2.1 by discharge. Due to deconditioning and steroid-related adrenal suppression, she was evaluated by physical therapy and discharged to inpatient rehab. Her diabetes was managed with insulin in the hospital, and metformin was restarted at discharge. She was transitioned from IV lasix to oral torsemide and continued all home immunosuppressive and supportive therapies.",
    "problems": [
